## Behavioral rules

**Think before coding.** State your assumptions. When the request is ambiguous, ask. When
several readings are reasonable, present them — do not pick one silently.

**Simplicity first.** The minimum that solves the stated problem. No speculative
generality, no abstraction with a single implementation.

**Surgical changes.** Change what the task requires. Do not improve adjacent code, do not
reformat files you touched for other reasons, do not rename on the way past.

**Goal-directed execution.** Define verifiable success criteria before starting, and
iterate until they are met.

These bias toward caution over speed. On trivial work, use judgment and say that you
shortened the flow.

## Quality gate

- **Behavior changed** — logic, business rules, state, an API contract — **then a test is
  written or updated.** Moving a file or changing a colour does not need a new test.
- **Run `{{TEST_COMMAND}}` at the end of every task and report the real result.** Never
  claim done without running it. If it fails, fix it or say so.
- No coverage percentage targets. A test earns its place by being behavioral, specific,
  deterministic and non-duplicative.

